I want to elaborate a bit to shed some light on that since some comments
in this thread are, at least, misleading.

The ASCII character set requires a 7 bit encoding. If you have characters
with codes that have the 8th bit set, then you have non-ASCII characters.
As others already pointed out, in your posting there were codes with
hexadecimal values A6 and B7, both beyond the ASCII code range (00-7F).
What these characters look like depends on the character set defined in
the newsreaders. You can only rely on ASCII coded values being widely and
unambiguously readable.

To give you an example: In ISO 8859-1 (ISO Latin 1) the code B7 is the
middle dot and the code A6 is the vertical broken bar. In ISO 8859-15
(ISO Latin 9), while the code B7 is also the middle dot, the code A6 is
the capital letter S with caron.
BTW: Since the euro currency sign has been introduced in Europe (which
is not present in ISO 8859-1), the ISO 8859-15 codeset is preferred.

So while your newsreader reads your own (maybe Latin 1) coded text well,
someone with a Latin 9 configured newsreader will see garbage.

There's no problem if we restrict to ASCII which is the common subset of
the most important actual (western) coded character sets.

